"\n    \n\n\n\n## Review\nThe biggest feature of this work is that it boldly discards the question  and doggedly digs into the question of .  \nThe culprit is revealed early in the novel, and the readers' finding the culprit is intentionally removed to force the reader's gaze to a more complex and fundamental mystery: tracking the motive.  \n\nIt unfolds in a unique format of presenting the records of two characters interchangeably rather than an omniscient author's perspective.  \nThe intersection of the culprit's  and Detective Kaga's  is a core theme of the novel, exploring the truth and fiction inherent in the act of recording itself.  \nThe reader must discern the greater truth hidden behind the recorded truth through two conflicting texts.  \nMalice means pure hatred that seeks only to negate and destroy the opponent's existence itself without any specific occasion or reason.  \n\nPersonally, it's a work I like."